   "source": [
    "### Imputing Missing Values – Urine Output\n",
    "At this stage, we handled missing values in the urine_output column, which represents the patient's urine output during their ICU stay.\n",
    "\n",
    "Only a subset of patients had documented urine measurements during their ICU stay. As a result, approximately 52% of the rows in this column were missing (NaN).\n",
    "\n",
    "There were several outliers, including a maximum value exceeding 200,000 mL.\n",
    "The mean was very high (11,857 mL), while the median (3,460 mL) better reflected the typical patient.\n",
    "The distribution was highly right-skewed, making the mean an unreliable measure of central tendency.\n",
    "\n",
    "We chose to impute missing values using the median, which is more robust to outliers, suitable for non-normal distributions, and helps preserve the underlying structure of the data."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 21,
   "id": "fa72c69f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "median per death:\n",
      " died_in_icu\n",
      "0    3460.0\n",
      "1    3020.0\n",
      "Name: urine_output, dtype: float64\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "median_by_death = icu_data.groupby('died_in_icu')['urine_output'].median()\n",
    "print(\"median per death:\\n\", median_by_death)\n",
    "\n",
    "icu_data['urine_output'] = icu_data.apply(\n",
    "    lambda row: median_by_death[row['died_in_icu']] if pd.isna(row['urine_output']) else row['urine_output'],\n",
    "    axis=1\n",
    ")\n"
   ]
  },
